TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42967089
TI  - Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction Following Significant Weight Loss During Tirzepatide Therapy: A Case Report.
AU  - Roy Aparajita
AU  - Pabani Umesh Kumar
AU  - Kyriakou Argyris
AU  - Elliott Mark K
JO  - Cureus
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.7759/cureus.117278
AB  - Tirzepatide is a dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist that produces substantial weight loss and improves multiple cardiometabolic risk factors. Current evidence supports its cardiovascular safety, with no established association between licensed tirzepatide therapy and acute coronary syndrome (ACS). As its clinical use continues to expand, reporting unusual cardiovascular presentations remains important for pharmacovigilance. We present a case of a 48-year-old South Asian man with obesity, diet-controlled prediabetes, a history of smoking, and a family history of premature coronary artery disease who had been receiving tirzepatide for approximately 18 months, achieving a weight loss of approximately 30 kg. Following increased physical activity, he developed progressive exertional chest pain and dyspnoea. Electrocardiography demonstrated inferolateral ischaemic changes, and serial high-sensitivity troponin T measurements were consistent with a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI). Transthoracic echocardiography showed preserved left ventricular systolic function with inferior regional wall motion abnormalities. Coronary angiography revealed severe triple-vessel coronary artery disease involving the left anterior descending, left circumflex, and right coronary arteries. He subsequently underwent successful coronary artery bypass grafting with an uncomplicated postoperative recovery. Although a temporal association existed between prolonged tirzepatide therapy, substantial weight loss, and presentation with NSTEMI, the presence of advanced multivessel coronary artery disease suggests longstanding atherosclerosis rather than a direct adverse effect of tirzepatide. A more plausible explanation is that significant weight loss improved functional capacity, allowing increased physical activity that unmasked previously silent obstructive coronary artery disease. This interpretation is consistent with current evidence demonstrating favourable cardiovascular effects of tirzepatide while acknowledging that causality cannot be established from a single case. This case highlights the importance of thoroughly evaluating new exertional chest pain in patients receiving tirzepatide despite marked improvements in weight and metabolic health. Clinicians should remain vigilant for underlying coronary artery disease in individuals with traditional cardiovascular risk factors. This report describes a temporal association rather than a causal relationship and contributes to the growing body of real-world experience with tirzepatide.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42967089/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42962407
TI  - Incretin-based therapies in psychiatry: A landscape analysis of ongoing randomized trials registered on ClinicalTrials.gov.
AU  - Fagiolini Andrea
AU  - Cuomo Alessandro
JO  - Journal of psychiatric research
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.jpsychires.2026.10.039
AB  - Incretin-based therapies, including conventional glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) and GLP-1-containing co-agonists, are being repurposed for psychiatric disorders. The breadth and maturity of the randomized pipeline are easily misread. We searched ClinicalTrials.gov on 1 September 2026 for ongoing randomized trials in which a psychiatric disorder or addictive behaviour was central to recruitment and a psychiatric or behavioural outcome was registered as primary or co-primary. The search queried the intervention field for 56 named agents and development codes and all record fields for five mechanism phrases, with an automated audit for agents absent from the term list. Trials with metabolic endpoints in psychiatric populations, neurocognitive trials, and non-randomized studies were catalogued separately. Thirty-four ongoing randomized trials met the core criteria, with a planned enrolment of 8519 participants; 25 (74%) were recruiting, 21 were phase 2 and six phase 3. Three compounds accounted for the entire pipeline, and 17 trials (50%) tested a GLP-1-containing co-agonist. Targets were alcohol use disorder (14 trials; n = 3686), major depressive disorder (3; n = 2116), opioid use disorder (4; n = 1021), tobacco or nicotine use (5; n = 708), bipolar disorder (1; n = 400), cannabis (3; n = 206), methamphetamine (1; n = 162), cocaine (2; n = 115), and binge-eating disorder (1; n = 105). Twenty-nine trials concerned addiction or tobacco use, and seven trials of a single co-agonist from one sponsor accounted for 62% of planned enrolment. Six further trials in schizophrenia-spectrum disorders registered metabolic rather than psychiatric primary endpoints. The ClinicalTrials.gov-registered psychiatric pipeline is larger than previously described but remains concentrated in addiction, and almost all development outside addiction belongs to one manufacturer's programme, which is proceeding without a positive randomized result in any psychiatric indication. Half of all trials use co-agonists, so a positive result would not identify the responsible mechanism.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42962407/
